# Frontend Design

Derived from [Anthropic frontend-design](https://github.com/anthropics/skills/tree/main/skills/frontend-design) (Apache-2.0). Adapted for Selfie Showroom / `sober-child`. Full workflow: `.cursor/skills/frontend-design/SKILL.md`.

Work as a design lead hired for a distinctive point of view. Make opinionated palette, type, and layout choices specific to this brief, and take one aesthetic risk you can justify.

## Scope

- **Use** for new UI, visual redesign, typography, motion, and interface copy.
- **Skip** WP-CLI, enqueue-only plumbing, non-visual PHP, and one-line functional fixes.
- Implement only in `sober-child`. Parent `sober/`, plugins, and core are read-only.

## Paths

| Need | Where |
|------|--------|
| Tokens | `src/scss/abstracts/_variables.scss` (`$ss-*`) |
| Component CSS | `src/scss/components/_*.scss` → import in `src/scss/main.scss` |
| Motion | `src/js/` |
| Woo / VC markup | `woocommerce/`, `vc_templates/` |
| Enqueue fonts / assets | `functions.php` |
| Compiled CSS/JS | `assets/` — do not hand-edit; `npm run watch` / `npm run build` |

Stay compatible with Sober / Woo / WPBakery class names unless the brief replaces the identity. Load fonts from `functions.php`, not CSS `@import`.

## Ground it in the subject

If the brief is vague, name the subject, audience, and the page's single job before designing. Distinctive choices come from that world's materials and vernacular. Default subject: Selfie Showroom (fashion / showroom e-commerce) unless the brief says otherwise. Use real content, not lorem.

## Design principles

- The **hero is a thesis**: lead with the most characteristic thing in the subject's world. A big number + small label + gradient accent is the template answer — use it only if it is truly best.
- **Typography carries personality**: pair display and body deliberately; set a real type scale. Type treatment should be memorable, not a neutral vehicle.
- **Structure is information**: eyebrows, numbers, dividers must encode something true. `01 / 02 / 03` only if order is actually a sequence the reader needs.
- **Motion with intent**: one orchestrated moment beats scattered effects. Honor `prefers-reduced-motion`. Extra animation often reads as AI-generated.
- Match complexity to the vision. Maximalism needs craft; minimalism needs precision.

## Process (two passes)

AI defaults to avoid unless the brief asks for them: (1) cream `#F4F1EA` + serif + terracotta; (2) near-black + acid-green/vermilion; (3) broadsheet hairlines, zero radius, dense columns. The brief's own words always win.

1. **Plan** a compact token system: **color** (4–6 named hexes → `$ss-*`), **type** (display with restraint, body, optional utility), **layout** (one-sentence concept + ASCII wireframe), **signature** (the one thing the page is remembered by).
2. **Critique** the plan: if any part is the generic default you would produce for any similar page, revise it and say why. Then build from the plan only.
3. Watch CSS specificity: child classes should not cancel each other (e.g. `.section` vs `.cta` padding). Prefer child BEM-style classes over `!important` against parent Sober selectors.
4. Keep planning in thinking; show the user work you are confident will delight them.

## Restraint

Spend boldness in one place — the signature — and keep the rest quiet. Cut decoration that does not serve the brief. Quality floor: responsive to mobile, visible keyboard focus, reduced motion. Critique while building (screenshots if available); remove one accessory before shipping. Verify the changed flow in the browser, desktop and mobile.

## Interface writing

Copy is design material. Write from the user's side of the screen; name what people control, not the system. Active voice: "Save changes," not "Submit." Same action name through the flow. Errors explain what happened and how to fix it; empty states invite an action. Sentence case, no filler, one job per element. PHP strings: child text domain + escape on output.
